---
import PageShell from '../components/PageShell.astro';
---

<PageShell title="Introduction" layout="wide">
  <section class="intro-top">
    <aside class="intro-aside">
      <figure class="intro-photo-frame">
        <div class="intro-photo-placeholder" aria-label="Photo placeholder">
          <span>Photo</span>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <div class="intro-meta">
        <p class="intro-name">Amna Qureshi, M.S. Biology</p>
        <p class="intro-role">Assistant Professor of Practice / Curriculum Coordinator, Biology</p>
        <p class="intro-org">Doane University</p>
        <p class="intro-period">2024–2027</p>
      </div>
    </aside>

    <div class="prose intro-lead">
      <p>
        I joined Doane University in August 2024 as an Assistant Professor of Practice and Curriculum
        Coordinator in Biology. My professional background combines more than twelve years of teaching
        Biology and related sciences with extensive experience in online instruction, curriculum
        development, and educational content creation. I hold a Master of Science in Biology with a
        concentration in Neurobiology and Behavior and am Quality Matters certified. Before joining
        Doane, I taught Biology and Anatomy &amp; Physiology in both face-to-face and online environments
        at Tarrant County College and Dallas College, while also participating in online course
        development.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="intro-body prose">
    <p>
      My role at Doane is somewhat different from a traditional faculty appointment because it
      combines direct teaching with responsibility for the development and coordination of online
      Biology curriculum. I teach online Biology courses as needed, primarily BIO-125 and BIO-126,
      while also coordinating the development and delivery of Biology courses offered through the Open
      Learning Academy (OLA). In this capacity, I work with Biology faculty, Course Developers,
      Instructional Designers, adjunct faculty, OLA staff, and external vendors to develop, implement,
      evaluate, and improve online courses.
    </p>
    <p>
      The students I serve through OLA are primarily post-baccalaureate, non-residential students. Many
      are balancing coursework with employment, family responsibilities, military service, or
      enrollment at another institution. Most Biology courses are offered in an accelerated eight-week
      asynchronous format, with additional five- and sixteen-week self-paced options. This context
      significantly shapes my approach to teaching and course design.
    </p>
    <p>
      In an asynchronous environment, students cannot rely on the informal structures of a traditional
      classroom. There is no opportunity to begin each class by explaining what students should focus
      on, remind them of upcoming deadlines in person, or observe whether students appear confused or
      confident. I therefore view course design itself as an essential component of teaching.
      Organization, communication, instructional materials, assessments, and feedback must collectively
      provide students with the guidance they need to become successful independent learners.
    </p>
    <p>
      My work during this review period has therefore focused on two connected goals: creating
      effective learning experiences for the students I teach directly and improving the quality and
      consistency of online Biology education more broadly through curriculum coordination. This
      portfolio reflects my development in both areas, with particular emphasis on teaching, course
      design, assessment, and continuous improvement.
    </p>
  </section>
</PageShell>
